tests/boot — the machine booting real DOS from disk images

The images live in disks/freedos/ (git-ignored; FreeDOS 1.3 is GPL):

  disks/freedos/floppy/144m/   FD13-FloppyEdition.zip, 1.44M disks:
                               x86BOOT.img, x86DSK01..05.img
  disks/freedos/lite/          FD13-LiteUSB.zip (not used yet)
  disks/freedos/c.img          128 MB hard disk, FreeDOS installed (386), WP51
  disks/freedos/c286.img       the same installed on a 286

from https://www.ibiblio.org/pub/micro/pc-stuff/freedos/files/distributions/1.3/official/

c.img was made by the installer itself, on a blank image:

  dd if=/dev/zero of=disks/freedos/c.img bs=1m count=128
  F=disks/freedos/floppy/144m
  tools/expect.py -v -t 900 tests/boot/fdinstall.exp -- ./dos-monster -m 386 -W -T 920 \
      -fda $F/x86BOOT.img:$F/x86DSK01.img:$F/x86DSK02.img:$F/x86DSK03.img:$F/x86DSK04.img:$F/x86DSK05.img \
      -hda disks/freedos/c.img -boot a

It partitions (FDISK through INT 13h), reboots (the reset vector), formats,
writes the system, extracts all five diskettes (1544 files) and finishes;
fdinstall.exp answers N to the closing reboot. c286.img is the same with
-m 286 (the 8086 kernel), booted on a 286 by run.sh.

The first installs died after the extraction — a jump into data inside
XCOPY on a 286, a runaway on a 386. That was the JIT: INT n and CALL FAR
[mem] held the target CS:IP in scratch registers across the frame's
pushes, and a push onto memory that had held translated code (SLICEREX's,
freed, reused as the next program's stack) fired the SMC check, whose
thunk did not save them. tests/dos/smcfar.com is the reduction.

Configuration changed on c.img (mtools, CR LF kept): FDCONFIG.SYS gets
MENU 6 — HIMEMX with DOS=HIGH, no JEMM (JEMMEX/JEMM386 need V86 mode,
which the machine does not have yet) — as the default, with a 1-second
menu (MENUDEFAULT=6,1); FDAUTO.BAT skips the CD-ROM probe for every
entry (there is no IDE controller, and UDVD2 waits out its timeouts on
four status ports — 8 of the 14 s it took JEMMEX to reach WP). Boot to C:\> is ~3 s; MEM shows 16,448K XMS and "FreeDOS
is resident in the high memory area".

With the V86/paging campaign (milestones 1-2, the interpreter) the stock
menu entries work too: 2 (JEMMEX) runs DOS in virtual-8086 mode with
paging — UMBs, COMMAND and FDAPM loaded high, 629K conventional free — and
WordPerfect runs under it; 3 (JEMM386) adds 16M of EMS. V86 code and the
memory manager's flat ring 0 are translated (campaign milestones 4-5);
boot to WP's editing screen under JEMMEX takes ~6 s. The default stays
entry 6, the plainer machine.

WordPerfect was added with mtools:

  mcopy -s -i disks/freedos/c.img@@32256 disks/WP51 ::/

(the partition starts at sector 63). run.sh boots scratch copies, so the
image never changes under a test.

MS-DOS 6.22 (disks/msdos622/, git-ignored: your own diskettes,
msdos622-1..3.img, and c.img installed from them by msinstall.sh)
-----------------------------------------------------------------

  tests/boot/msinstall.sh            # ~25 s, blank disk to C:\> with C:\DOS

Setup (the upgrade edition) won't touch a blank disk, so the script exits
it, partitions with FDISK (it reboots through the reset vector), formats
with FORMAT C: /S, and lets Setup finish over the fresh system ("Continue
Setup and replace"). Diskette changes are expect.py's ESC + key. run.sh
boots it as Setup left it (HIMEM, DOS=HIGH), then with EMM386.EXE RAM,
DOS=HIGH,UMB and LH SMARTDRV (V86 mode, paging: 16M EMS, UMBs), then
WordPerfect under that.

What it took, beyond FreeDOS:
- IO.SYS times the keyboard controller's ACK by counting port 61h's
  refresh toggle (bit 4, every 15 us), and wants the FAh ACK itself.
- INT 16h AH=00h can't wait in the host once INT 9 is hooked (IO.SYS,
  KEYB): it returns unfinished with IF set and the INT reruns.
- IO.SYS runs a stack on its own code: a CALL FAR's push into the running
  block must not leave the block mid-instruction (the SMC check of a block
  ender names no running block).
- EMM386 looks for its page frame in open bus: C0000-EFFFF read FFh and
  ignore writes (X86_BM_EMPTY), and a not-present gate that also has a
  bad type is #GP, not #NP (QEMU as the oracle: -d int).
- The BIOS's INT 8 chains INT 1Ch through a real stub (INT 1Ch; IRET at
  F100:0000), so a V86 monitor reflects it like any other interrupt.
- POST sets the tick count from the RTC, so TIME is right at boot.

DOOM (shareware, disks/doom) under MS-DOS: run.sh copies it onto the
EMM386 image and onto a plain HIMEM one. Under EMM386, DOS/4GW enters
protected mode through VCPI and builds its own page tables; without it,
it switches itself over XMS memory. Both needed:
- a real master 8259: ICW1-4 (the vector base), OCW3 (port 20h reads the
  IRR, or the ISR after 0Bh) and a mask that is honoured. DOS/4GW under
  VCPI tells IRQ 0 from a double fault, both vector 8, by the ISR; a port
  20h that read 0 made the first tick "exception 08h (double fault)".
- the JIT fetching a paged code page mapped BELOW its linear address
  (DOOM at 498000 on physical 3ED000): the decode added the 32-bit
  physical-minus-linear delta to the host pointer unwrapped, 4 GB past
  guest memory. pgtest's T0E runs code on such a page.

Windows 3.11 (disks/win311/, git-ignored: your OEM 3.5" diskettes
in oem/DISK1-6.IMG, and c.img installed from them)
--------------------------------------------------------------------

  tests/boot/wininstall.sh           # ~3 min: MS-DOS disk + Setup, end to end

The diskettes are flattened into C:\WININST (no CD-ROM on the machine;
Setup finds each disk's files by the DISKn markers). Setup's second half
is Windows itself in standard mode, drawn in VGA mode 12h, so the script
drives it blind with expect.py's "send" and "delay"; "shot FILE" saves
the picture (X86_SCREEN_PNG, rewritten once a second) for looking at.
run.sh starts "win /s" and checks Program Manager's title bar pixel
(tools/pngpix.py); then plain "win", 386 enhanced mode, opens an MS-DOS
Prompt (a V86 VM beside Windows, full screen), runs VER there and EXITs
back to Program Manager. Inside the VM MEM shows 581K free and 1024K of
EMS, WIN386's own.

What standard mode needed:
- an inward CALL through a gate or an interrupt reading its new stack
  from a 286 TSS (SP0 at 2, SS0 at 4, four bytes a ring). DOSX has one;
  the 386 layout read a null SS, and the #SS handler's own CALL faulted
  on it forever. A null SS there is #TS now.
- VGA modes 0Dh/0Eh/10h/12h: planar from the mode set, drawn through
  the attribute palette (pc_vga_frame reports its size; the window and
  -G follow).
- translated reads of the VGA window while the planar VGA answers them:
  blocks translated then send such reads (and MOVS/LODS/CMPS/SCAS) to
  the interpreter, which loads the latches; cpu->dev_hook retranslates
  when the planar form comes or goes. Without it Windows' fills and
  copies through the latches drew nothing.
- scripted keys for a program that reads IRQ 1 but calls INT 16h now
  and then: fed after 250 ms without BIOS polling, not only after total
  silence (Windows took the first key and no more).

What 386 enhanced mode needed — WIN386 virtualizes the VGA, the PIC and
the keyboard, and keeps its virtual state from the I/O it traps, so the
BIOS has to do its I/O as instructions, not from the host:
- INT 10h AH=00 programs the mode's registers by OUTs (tools/vgabios.asm,
  the native half, from standard VGA parameter tables at F100:0400). The
  VDD read the CRTC back to learn the screen's geometry; the host's own
  writes left its copy at the previous mode ("Video initialization
  failed"). The tables themselves were new: every register of modes 0-7,
  0Dh-13h, misc output at 3C2h/3CCh.
- INT 8's EOI is the stub's OUT 20h, and a booted machine's INT 9 is a
  native stub (F100:0010): IN 60h, the host's translation (trap F5h, code
  in AL), OUT 20h. The host EOI left IRQ 0 in service in the VM's virtual
  PIC, which then delivered nothing below it — no keys.
- the 8042: status bit 0 while a scancode waits (VKD looks before it
  reads), the command byte (read 20h, write 60h), self tests, AD/AE, and
  only 60h/D1h-D4h take a data byte: command 20h had been swallowing the
  keyboard's F3h, which then never got its ACK. The keyboard answers
  FF/F2/EE/F4/F5 and ACKs a command's data byte.
- ROM fonts in guest memory (F000:C000/D000/E000, FA6E; INT 1Fh, 43h) for
  INT 10h AX=1130h's ES:BP.
- the display follows the VGA registers, not the BIOS data area's mode
  byte, which WIN386 keeps per VM: the picture flickered between the DOS
  VM's text and Windows' mode 12h as the VMs took turns.

Enhanced mode runs at ~35 MIPS: Windows' segmented 16-bit protected-mode
code under paging is the interpreter's (only flat paged code and V86 are
translated). That is the next speed campaign.

tests/boot/bench.sh prints the milestones the speed campaign tracks
(wall clock, this machine): power-on to C:\>, then "win" to a drawn
Program Manager in each mode. At 2026-09-23 after the A20 change:

  win /s   C:\> at  2.52 s   Program Manager  0.82 s later
  win      C:\> at  2.52 s   Program Manager  1.33 s later

(2 s of the 2.52 is MS-DOS's own "Starting MS-DOS..." F5/F8 pause.)
Before it, C:\> took 12.6 s: HIMEM tests extended memory flipping A20
around each chunk, ~14500 times, and every flip flushed the whole
translation cache. The A20 state is now part of the block key
(KEY_A20OFF), and a flip flushes nothing.

Linux: tests/boot/tcimage.sh builds disks/linux/tc.img from Tiny Core
16.2's ISO (Core-16.2.iso, from distro.ibiblio.org/tinycorelinux over
HTTPS): a 64 MB disk with the kernel and initramfs on a FAT16 partition,
GRUB 2 (Homebrew's i686-elf-grub) in the MBR and the gap after it. The
boot test runs it on -m 586 -mem 128 to the tc@box prompt and uname -a
(about two minutes, most of it Tiny Core's own start-up). Linux's atkbd
needed the keyboard's command replies to get through while scanning is
off (F5h), and its console scrolls by moving the CRTC start address,
which the text dump expect.py reads now follows.

The Pentium's APICs (pc/pc_apic.c): the local APIC at FEE00000h and an
I/O APIC at FEC00000h, announced by MP Specification 1.4 tables at
F000:F000 (the processor, the buses, the I/O APIC's inputs: the 8259s'
INTR as ExtINT on 0, the PIT on 2, the other ISA lines on their own
numbers, PCI cards on 16-19, level). With them Linux goes to symmetric
I/O mode: /proc/interrupts shows the timer on "IO-APIC 2-edge", the
keyboard controller on 1, the disk on 14, the e1000 on 19 ("PCI->APIC
IRQ transform: INT A -> IRQ 19"), and the local APIC's timer is its
clock event device (clockevent0: lapic; the PIT's count stops after
calibration). Linux without the tables enables the local APIC but
never sets up its timer ("virtual wire mode setup with no
configuration"), and with the tables but no I/O APIC it complains of
"no explicit IRQ entries". The images' kernel command lines used to say
noapic; they no longer do. -noapic makes a Pentium without either.

More than one processor (-smp N, pc/pc_smp.c): the bootstrap processor
is the main thread as before; each application processor is a host
thread with its own x86_cpu and translator (JIT buffer, block cache,
TLB) over the one guest memory, its own local APIC, and the machine's
devices behind one recursive lock — port I/O, memory-mapped devices,
the BIOS's host services and the device half of pc_poll. An AP waits
for its STARTUP IPI on a condition variable, starts at vector:0000 in
real mode, and when halted sleeps until an IPI, an I/O APIC routing or
its own APIC timer; INIT sends it back to waiting. The MP table lists
them all. Linux 6.12 (Tiny Core) brings up both ("smp: Brought up 1
node, 2 CPUs"), spreads devices over them through the I/O APIC's
lowest-priority delivery, and its reschedule, function-call and TLB
shootdown IPIs flow both ways; a fork storm and parallel shell loops
run. Measured by the guest's own clock (date +%s around the loops):
on two processors one shell loop 4 s, two at once 5 s, four 11 s; on
four processors 3, 5 and 7 s. Real parallelism, well short of linear:
the per-poll machine lock and the sticky-bitmap sweeps are the
suspects for the speed pass. (The threads ask macOS for a performance
core — the QoS hint in ap_thread — or an application processor lands
on an efficiency core at half speed.) A measurement trap: an expect
pattern like "TWO=" matches the echoed command the moment it is typed,
so the next command was sent while the loops still ran; the markers
are now spelled so that only the output matches. The boot suite boots
it to the shell on two.

What SMP changed underneath:
- Bus-locked instructions (LOCK prefix, XCHG with memory, CMPXCHG8B)
  leave translated code for the interpreter, which does them with the
  host's atomics on the guest byte itself (a compare-and-swap loop, an
  exchange, an 8-byte CAS). The first version serialized them with a
  spinlock around a plain read, compute and write, and the kernel's
  queued spinlock deadlocked within seconds: its unlock is a plain
  byte store from the other processor, which landed between the read
  and the write and was put back stale. Hardware's locked cycle
  excludes other stores entirely; the CAS loop does too. The spinlock
  remains only for what the atomics cannot do (unaligned, straddling
  a page, device memory).
- Memory ordering. x86 is TSO; the ARM host is not, and it showed: a
  message-passing litmus test (tools/litmus.asm: P0 stores x then y,
  P1 loads y then x; y=1 with x=0 is impossible on x86) saw 8, 4 and
  9 forbidden outcomes per two million tries. In SMP mode translated
  code's guest loads are RCpc acquire loads (LDAPR) and its stores
  release stores (STLR), together exactly TSO, and the interpreter's
  bytes are acquire/release too; the litmus test then saw 0, 0, 0.
  Those forms fault across a 16-byte line (within one, FEAT_LSE2
  allows any misalignment); a SIGBUS handler does such an access with
  a fence and steps the PC. The boot's initramfs inflate crosses lines
  1.4 million times; steady state hardly ever. X86_NO_TSO=1 turns the
  forms off, for measurement.
- Serializing instructions (CPUID, IRET, INVD, WBINVD, INVLPG, LGDT,
  LIDT, LLDT, LTR, WRMSR, MOV to CR) end the block in SMP mode, so
  code another processor changed is seen from the point where the
  architecture promises it.
- A store into translated code invalidates the writer's own blocks as
  before and queues the address for every other translator, which
  sweeps it at its next block boundary (its run is cut short by
  jit_cur_hit). The code bitmap's bits stay set in SMP mode, since any
  translator may hold blocks there, at the cost of spurious sweeps on
  pages that were once code. A translator reading a page while another
  processor writes it is a race a few microseconds wide, accepted for
  now. Translation itself is serialized (the backend's statics).
- Interrupts, IPIs and invalidations reach a processor at a block
  boundary: the quantum is 65536 instructions in SMP mode (a million
  otherwise).
- The kernel logs one WARNING at the second processor's bring-up, in
  cpuid-deps clearing X86_BUG_F00F (bit 704 of the 24 feature words)
  on a family-5 secondary; QEMU's -cpu pentium -smp 2 logs the same
  one at the same address. The kernel's, for a dual Pentium.
- The 586 CPU test and -V stay single-processor. -smp needs the
  Pentium and the JIT.

Haiku on two processors needs -acpi. Its boot loader tries ACPI's MADT
first and the MP tables second, and its MP parser compares the
configuration table's signature against a byte-swapped constant
('PCMP' where the floating pointer's is written '_PM_' to match
memory): every specification-conformant table is "invalid config table
signature", a bug real machines never show because they have ACPI
(headers/private/kernel/arch/x86/arch_smp.h, still so in master). So
pc/pc_acpi.c lays the least ACPI at F000:F400: RSDP, RSDT, a FADT with
an empty DSDT, a FACS and PM1a blocks on a quiet I/O range (B000h),
and the MADT with the processors, the I/O APIC, the IRQ 0 override and
the NMI line. Off by default: an installer that sees ACPI picks its
ACPI HAL and expects the rest (Windows 2000). With it Haiku's kernel
says "found 2 logical cpus", uses the APIC timer and the I/O APIC, and
routes the disk's IRQ 14 to the second processor — which found one
more single-processor assumption: the IDE model's command deadline was
the bootstrap processor's instruction count, polled by its thread
alone, so a command from the other processor completed only when the
first woke from its idle nap, and Haiku's driver had timed out. With
more than one processor the deadline is a time, any processor's poll
completes it, and a nap is capped while a command is in flight. The
welcome window comes at about 70 s; the boot suite checks it. Linux
reads the same tables ("Using ACPI (MADT) for SMP configuration
information") and boots as before; its only remarks are about the
global lock and the buttons the empty DSDT does not provide.

Windows 2000 on two processors (issue #11, in progress). The installed
system is a uniprocessor one; without reinstalling, its D: got
ntkrnlmp.exe and halmps.dll expanded from the CD's I386 (7zz opens the
LZX cabinets) and a boot.ini entry with /KERNEL=ntkrnlmp.exe
/HAL=halmps.dll, then the MP copies of ntdll.dll, kernel32.dll and
win32k.sys (I386 and DRIVER.CAB; the install's are lock-prefix patched
for one processor, F0 to 90 at the sites in their load-config tables,
and the MP kernel stops with UP_MP_IMAGE_MISMATCH on them). What it
found, each fixed:
- The MPS HAL's clock is the RTC through the I/O APIC (vector D1) and
  its IRQL is the TPR. A TPR write or an EOI that makes an already
  requested vector deliverable now makes translated code leave at the
  next block boundary (pc_apic.c update), as a new request always did;
  before, the kernel lowered IRQL to let a requested APC interrupt fire
  and translated code ran on for up to a quantum, so an I/O completion
  APC wrote its status block into a frame long returned from: STOP 7F,
  EBP = 0 at a LEAVE, on one processor as on two.
- An interpreted instruction that turns IF on with an interrupt
  waiting (POPFD, IRET) is followed by a poll at once, not when the
  run loop's countdown says: the HAL's KeTryToAcquireQueuedSpinLock
  loop (pushf; cli; try; popf) had IF clear at every counted poll, its
  IPI never landed, and the sender spun forever.
- Aligned words and doublewords are single-copy atomic in the
  interpreter under SMP (x86_rd/x86_wr), and page-table entries are
  read whole with their accessed and dirty bits set atomically
  (x86_paging.c). Byte by byte, the kernel's queued spinlock read a
  successor link of 000000DC — the low byte of FFDFF4DC had landed —
  and handed the lock to address DC (both processors then waited on
  it); a torn page-table entry or an accessed-bit OR putting an old low
  byte back over a new entry is the same class.
- A store into a watched descriptor flushes every translator, not only
  the writer's.
Found with: X86_WATCH_PHYS / X86_WATCH_PAGE (physical dwords or pages
kept off translated code's fast paths, every load and store printed
with a sequence number, processor, EIP and value; the spin reads
de-duplicated), X86_TRACE_CASFAIL (a failed CMPXCHG at an address
single-steps the next 40 instructions), X86_INTERP_FROM / X86_WATCH_MEM
and X86_WATCH_EBP under -V, X86_INTERP_CPU (a processor list kept in
the interpreter, for bisection), X86_DUMP_BLOCK (a block's host code),
X86_EXC_TRACE's @lin filter, and sample(1) on the host process.
tools/qlock.asm is the kernel's queued spinlock protocol, instruction
for instruction, as a two-processor Linux test (count 2N or HANG with
the lock state); it passes, under contention and with gaps. Where it
stands: the MP kernel and HAL boot to the desktop on one processor,
and reached it once on two (with the second processor interpreted);
other runs stop in different places (STOP 1E reading address 8 or 21,
STOP D1 at EIP 8, winlogon terminated), so something still tears or
goes stale between the processors.

tests/boot/tcroot.sh builds disks/linux/tcroot.img: the same kernel on a
small FAT16 partition, and Tiny Core's userland unpacked from core.gz onto
an ext2 partition (mke2fs -d, then debugfs making every inode root's, as
the initramfs has them; devtmpfs.mount=1 fills /dev, which a non-root
unpack cannot). GRUB boots root=/dev/sda2 with no initramfs, so every file
after the kernel comes through the IDE controller. The boot test writes
/hello.txt as root and reads it back out of the image with debugfs.

The network: tc.img again, with -nic e1000,user — the 82545EM with
libslirp's NAT behind it. Tiny Core's udhcpc gets 10.0.2.15 at boot; the
test pings slirp's gateway (10.0.2.2) twice, so nothing outside the host
is involved. By hand, nslookup and wget reach the Internet, and 2-3 MB
each way over TCP to a listener on the host (10.0.2.2 is the host's
loopback) came through byte-exact at 13-21 MB/s — Linux's e1000 uses the
card's checksum insertion and TSO for what it sends, and gets the FCS on
what it receives. Two things it took besides the card:
- the TSC counted instructions, and Linux, having no other clocksource
  on this machine, kept it even after its watchdog called it unstable: a
  guest idle in HLT runs no instructions, so its clock stood still, and
  sleep 2 (or ping's second packet) waited forever. The TSC now runs on
  the machine's clock at 200 MHz (cpu->tsc_clock; -V replays its
  readings to the shadow).
- libslirp before config version 6 calls register_poll_fd without
  looking: a NULL there crashed the first UDP socket it opened (DNS).

Slackware 3.1 ("Slackware 96": Linux 2.0.0, gcc 2.7.2, XFree86 3.1.2),
installed by its own setup. tests/boot/slackimage.sh makes the install
disk from a copy of the release (https://mirrors.slackware.com/slackware/
slackware-3.1/: slakware/, kernels/bare.i/zImage, rootdsks/color.gz, into
disks/slack31/): 504 MB, 1015x16x63, with a FAT16 partition holding the
disk sets as \SLAKWARE\A1 ... (INSTALL.TXT 3.4.2.2's "from an MS-DOS
partition"), a Linux one and swap. The machine has no floppy controller,
so GRUB 2 plays LOADLIN: bare.i with the root disk as its initrd. Then
tests/boot/slackinstall.sh answers setup's dialogs (about 45 minutes,
most of it gzip and tar in 16 MB) — A AP D E F N X XAP Y, LILO in the
MBR — and the boot test starts from the BIOS: LILO, Linux from hda2, a
root login, a C program compiled and run.
- The mirror has lost four packages (procmail, seyon, xfileman, xv).
  xap4 held only xv: left empty, setup's "*.tgz" matched nothing and it
  stopped for the literal name. The image leaves the disk out and moves
  its install.end to xap3.
- Linux 2.0 blanks the console after ten idle minutes, and a prompt that
  comes after the packages is then invisible to expect.py; the install
  script turns blanking off first. (By hand, Alt+F1 unblanks it without
  typing anything into the dialog: the kernel keeps console switches.)
- expect.py takes a script line starting with # for a comment: a root
  prompt's pattern is [#].

ReactOS 0.4.16, installed by its own setup. tests/boot/rosinstall.sh
boots the release ISO (disks/reactos/ReactOS-0.4.16-i386.iso, a hybrid
image: an MBR over the ISO 9660 filesystem) as the second hard disk,
-boot d, with a blank 2 GB first disk, drives the text-mode setup from
its screens and the wizard blind, and the boot test takes the result to
the desktop. What ReactOS asked of the machine:
- Its ATA port driver (new in 0.4.16) finds IDE channels only under a PCI
  IDE function, or a PnP BIOS's *PNP0600; the PCI machine (-pci) has the
  channel as a PIIX3 function at 00:01.0.
- FreeLdr asks for PCI through the real-mode PCI BIOS, INT 1Ah AH=B1h —
  the 32-bit one behind BIOS32 was all there was, so the kernel heard of
  no PCI bus and started no bus driver.
- Its i8042 driver resets the mouse with both interfaces disabled in the
  command byte and polls for the ACK, which the 8042 had held back; its
  kernel debugger polls port 60h with interrupts off, which lost every
  key after the first (the pending IRQ 1 held the latch).
- Its display driver for a PCI display (display.inf, PCI\CC_0300) is
  vgapnp.sys (the VBE miniport, vbemp) with framebuf.dll, and -vbe puts
  the display at 00:02.0 for it; without a PCI display the Root\VgaSave
  fallback is 640x480x16. The desktop is 800x600x32. The VBE probe is
  INT 10h from the kernel's V86 monitor (Ke386CallBios) in CSRSS's
  address space, with the buffer at 2000:0000 in a page videoprt
  re-protects between calls: a transition PTE, not present, that a real
  BIOS's first touch faults back in. Our host-side services now do the
  same — pc_hle_touch raises #PF at the stub for a guest buffer it
  cannot reach, and the stub runs again when the handler returns. And
  vbemp, a VBE 3.0 reader, takes the colour masks from the linear-mode
  fields of the mode info block (36h), which were two bytes off.
- At 16 colours (VgaSave: vga.sys with vgaddi.dll, the fallback without
  -vbe, and the setup wizard) ReactOS draws striped box fills, garbled
  small bitmaps, no desktop icons and a striped taskbar. That is its
  vgaddi, not our planar VGA: the same disk image booted with /BASEVIDEO
  on QEMU (-vga std) and on dos-monster renders the same 640x480x16
  screen identically, dotted garbage along a dialog's bottom edge
  included, to the pixel (the only differences are QEMU's 6-bit DAC
  rounding, 87 for our 85). On QEMU's ISA VGA (-vga none -device
  isa-vga) its vgaddi bugchecks outright (STOP 1E in vgaddi.dll).
- The copy stage trips its FAT driver's assertion now and then
  (CORE-19741) and waits for a debugger: run the install again. Its
  kernel debug log, with the "ReactOS (Debug)" boot entry, is plain text
  on -com1.

FreeDOS on the network: -nic ne2000,user, a Novell NE2000 (the DP8390;
pc/pc_ne2000.c) on the ISA bus at 300h, IRQ 3, with slirp behind it.
The software is FreeDOS 1.3's own, from its repository's net group
(https://www.ibiblio.org/pub/micro/pc-stuff/freedos/files/repositories/
1.3/net/: crynwr.zip and mtcp.zip, into disks/freedos/net): Crynwr's
NE2000.COM packet driver from 1993 ("ne2000 0x60 3 0x300") and mTCP,
whose DHCP gets 10.0.2.15. The boot test pings slirp's gateway. By hand,
HTGET fetched a page off the Internet, and 2 MB from an HTTP server on
the host (10.0.2.2) came through byte-exact at about 160 KB/s; -V is
clean through the driver, DHCP and ping (the card has no DMA: everything
crosses its ports, which -V's log already replays).
